      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1: Identify structure A.</strong><br/>Structure A designates the lobulated, blackish mass containing mature ova situated in close proximity to each kidney; these are the ovaries, which have no functional connection with the kidneys.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Identify structure B.</strong><br/>Structure B marks the long, coiled, glandular tube opening anteriorly near the lung by an oviducal funnel and discharging posteriorly into the cloaca; this is the oviduct.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Identify structure C.</strong><br/>Structure C points to the slender duct running from the lateral border of the kidney straight to the cloaca; in female frogs, these ureters transport only urine and open separately into the cloaca without carrying any reproductive products.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Identify structure D.</strong><br/>Structure D indicates the terminal slit-like external vent situated at the posterior end of the cloaca through which urine, faeces, and ova are released into the water; this is the cloacal aperture.</p>
<p><strong>Step 5: Final conclusion and conceptual answer.</strong><br/>Synthesizing the identifications: A represents the ovary, B represents the oviduct, C represents the ureter, and D represents the cloacal aperture.</p>]]></text>
